# Audit - Target B explicit general coherent-phase interpolation

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Candidate:** `docs/reports/target_b_explicit_general_phase_interpolation_2026-09-05.md`  
**Verdict:** PASS under its stated dominant coherent-component hypotheses.  
**Global LEVEL I status:** STILL OPEN.

## 1. Scope

The candidate considers one coherent three-phase component with class sizes

`M>=b>=a`,

`t=b/M>=tau>0`, `s=a/M`,

and local Theorem-51 deficit

`Delta=[M(a+b)-E]/M^2`.

It assumes the same strong component-scale hypotheses used by the previously audited explicit phase-gap theorems: triangular phase coherence, no transversal triangle, `o(M^2)` crossings outside the component, `T=O(M)`, and the component carries the global quadratic rhombus core at its scale (`R=E+o(M^2)`).

The theorem is therefore a **local dominant-component interpolation theorem**, not a multi-grain global theorem.

## 2. Kappa-free largest/second pair missing floor

Write

`e_AB=Mb-x`, `mu=x/(Mb)`.

The candidate reuses only the geometric part of the explicit unique-max proof. That part does not use a positive class gap `kappa`.

If `mu<=mu_0=8*10^(-5)`, corrected dual-tree extraction gives a complete `A' x B'` grid with total two-phase omission, normalized by `M`,

`L<=3sqrt(mu)+mu+o(1)`.

The trimmed-grid star theorem gives the full ideal diagonal function `F(s,t)` after optimizing the entire coherent third-phase cancellation. Its uniform margin is

`F(s,t)-c(s+t)>=gamma_0`,

`gamma_0=3/4-c/2=0.1986711045...`.

The omitted `A/B` trains are charged once for shortening the coherent sums and once for arbitrary signed diagonal contribution, giving

`D^2/M^2>=F(s,t)-4sqrt(3)L-o(1)`.

At `mu_0`, the loss is below `0.1865`, strictly below `gamma_0`. Therefore the lower diameter bound exceeds `c(s+t)` by a fixed margin.

The dominant-component assumption gives

`n/M^2<=s+t+o(1)`,

so the strict translated-lattice comparison gives the incompatible upper bound

`D^2/M^2<c(s+t)+o(1)`.

Thus

`liminf x/(Mb)>=8*10^(-5)`

is valid without any lower bound on `1-b/M`.

PASS.

## 3. Transversal-triple algebra

Write

`e_AC=Ma-y`, `e_BC=z`.

No transversal triangle gives

`Mz<=ax+by`.

Hence

`Delta M^2=x+y-z`

`>=(1-s)x+(1-t)y`

`>=(1-s)x`.

Using the missing floor `x>=(mu_0-o(1))Mb=(mu_0 t-o(1))M^2` and `t>=tau` gives

`1-s<=Delta/(mu_0 tau)+o(1)`.

Since `s<=t<=1`, this also controls `1-t` and `t-s`.

PASS.

## 4. Random balanced restriction

Choose uniformly random subsets `A_0 subset A`, `B_0 subset B` of size `a` and retain all of `C`.

The survival probabilities are

- `A-B`: `s^2/t`;
- `A-C`: `s`;
- `B-C`: `s/t`.

Because `0<=s<=t<=1`, both latter probabilities are at least `s^2/t`. Therefore

`E[E_0]>=(s^2/t)E`,

so some deterministic restriction has this crossing count.

Since `a^2=s^2M^2`,

`E_0/a^2>=E/(tM^2)=(s+t-Delta)/t`.

Thus the balanced deficit obeys

`epsilon_bal<= (t-s+Delta)/t`

`<= (1-s+Delta)/tau`

`<= [Delta/(mu_0 tau)+Delta]/tau+o(1)`.

PASS.

## 5. Balanced combinatorial stability is used within scope

The explicit balanced combinatorial lemma is graph-theoretic: a balanced tripartite graph with no transversal triangle and deficit `epsilon_bal` has a phase pair with missing density at most

`4sqrt(epsilon_bal)`.

The selected phase pair remains internally noncrossing because it is a subset of coherent phase classes of the original train component. The corrected dual-tree theorem therefore applies and gives complete-grid omission

`L_grid<=6 epsilon_bal^(1/4)+4sqrt(epsilon_bal)+o(1)`.

PASS.

## 6. All trains deleted by balancing are restored

The random restriction deletes exactly

`(M-a)+(b-a)`

original trains from the `A,B` classes. This is at most

`2M(1-s)`.

Regardless of which two balanced classes supply the complete grid, every deleted original train is restored as an arbitrary signed train-coordinate contribution. Relative to the balanced scale `a=sM`, the candidate uses the safe bound

`L_del=2(1-s)/s`.

This covers all cases:

- selected pair `A_0-B_0`, third phase `C`;
- selected pair `A_0-C`, third phase `B_0`;
- selected pair `B_0-C`, third phase `A_0`.

The unselected balanced phase class receives its full coherent cancellation through `F(1,1)=3`. No original train is omitted without charge.

PASS.

## 7. External-component error

Under the contradiction threshold, the algebra forces `s->1`, hence `a=Theta(M)` with `a/M->1`. The candidate hypothesis says crossings involving trains outside the coherent component are `o(M^2)=o(a^2)`.

After a fixed positive central trim of the extracted complete grid, TG1 penetration depth is `Theta(a)`. Therefore only `o(a)` genuinely external trains can separate the selected diagonal. Sending the trim fraction to zero after the asymptotic limit gives the stated `o(a)` error.

PASS.

## 8. Numerical threshold

Assume

`Delta<4*10^(-17)tau^2`.

Then

`1-s<5*10^(-13)tau+o(1)<=5*10^(-13)+o(1)`,

so the deleted-original-train error is below `3*10^(-12)` eventually.

The balanced deficit satisfies

`epsilon_bal<6*10^(-13)+o(1)<10^(-12)`.

Hence

`L_grid<0.00601`.

The balanced grid lower bound gives

`D^2/a^2>3-4sqrt(3)(0.00601)-10^(-10)>2.95`.

For the full original dominant component,

`D^2/a^2<c(s+t-Delta)/s^2+o(1)`.

Since `s=1-o(1)`, `t<=1`, this is eventually below `2.21` (`2c=2.20531558...`). Contradiction.

The numerical slack is large; small rounding changes cannot affect the conclusion.

PASS.

## 9. Section-50 phase-ratio insertion

For fixed pruning threshold `lambda`, every retained vertex has degree at least `lambda D`. If `A` is the largest phase class and `B` the second largest, an `A` vertex has at most `a+b<=2b` possible phase neighbors. Therefore

`b>=lambda D/2`.

Since `M<=T<=C_TD`,

`t=b/M>=lambda/(2C_T)`.

Thus the theorem supplies an explicit local coefficient for every fixed dense-core regime.

PASS.

## 10. What is not proved

The theorem does **not** globalize across several macroscopic retained components. Its diameter upper comparison uses `R=E+o(M^2)` for the selected component. If several grains each carry a positive quadratic share of the global rhombi, one component's local crossing count does not determine global `n` and therefore does not provide the same local diameter upper bound.

This multi-grain local-to-global transfer remains the principal Target B obstruction.

## Verdict

The candidate theorem survives audit:

`Delta >= 4*10^(-17) tau^2-o(1)`

under its stated dominant coherent-component hypotheses.

It closes the previously explicit local `M>b` versus tied/balanced class-size interpolation gap. It does not settle Erdős #99.
